**Happy Coding with MongoDB! 🎉**5. Check [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) section above4. Review Prisma logs: `DEBUG="prisma:*" npm run dev`3. Test connection string with `mongosh`2. Verify IP address is whitelisted1. Check MongoDB Atlas cluster is running (not paused)If you encounter issues:**Need Help?**---- **MongoDB University** (Free Courses): https://university.mongodb.com/- **MongoDB Compass** (GUI): https://www.mongodb.com/products/compass- **Prisma MongoDB Guide**: https://www.prisma.io/docs/concepts/database-connectors/mongodb- **MongoDB Atlas**: https://www.mongodb.com/cloud/atlas- **MongoDB Documentation**: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/## Additional Resources---```npx prisma studio# 8. (Optional) Open Prisma Studionpm run dev# 7. Start development servernpx prisma db seed# 6. (Optional) Seed databasenpx prisma db push# 5. Push schema to MongoDBnpx prisma generate# 4. Generate Prisma Client# 3. Update DATABASE_URL in .env.localcp .env.example .env.local# 2. Copy environment templatenpm install# 1. Install dependencies```bash## Quick Start Commands---- ✅ Prisma Studio (GUI works identically)- ✅ Relations and nested queries- ✅ Prisma Client API (queries work the same)**What Stayed the Same:**- ✅ Migrations: Use `db push` instead of `migrate`- ✅ Relations: Same syntax, but use ObjectId references- ✅ Auto-increment: Removed (MongoDB uses ObjectId)- ✅ ID fields: `Int` → `String` with `@db.ObjectId`**What Changed:**### Migration Considerations| **Indexes** | B-tree, GiST, etc. | B-tree, text, 2d, etc. || **Transactions** | ACID compliant | ACID in 4.0+ || **Joins** | Native SQL joins | `$lookup` aggregation || **ID Type** | Integer/UUID | ObjectId (12-byte) || **Schema** | Strict, pre-defined | Flexible, dynamic || **Type** | Relational (SQL) | Document (NoSQL) ||---------|-----------|----------|| Feature | PostgreSQL | MongoDB |## MongoDB vs PostgreSQL: Key Differences---3. Wait 2-3 minutes for cluster to resume2. Click "Resume" on your cluster1. Go to MongoDB Atlas dashboard**Solution:**Free tier M0 clusters auto-pause after 60 days of inactivity.### MongoDB Atlas Cluster Paused```mongodb+srv://user:pass@cluster.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority# Incorrect (missing database name)mongodb+srv://user:pass@cluster.mongodb.net/DBNAME?retryWrites=true&w=majority# Correct format```bash3. For MongoDB Atlas, include `retryWrites=true&w=majority`2. Ensure database name is specified1. Check connection string format**Solution:**### Error: "P1013: The provided database string is invalid"```npx prisma generate```bashThen regenerate:```}  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")  provider = "mongodb"  // NOT "postgresql"datasource db {```prismaEnsure `prisma/schema.prisma` has correct provider:**Solution:**### Error: "Prisma Client: No valid provider found"```sudo systemctl enable mongod# Enable auto-start on bootsudo systemctl start mongod# Start MongoDBsudo systemctl status mongod# Check status```bash**Linux:**```brew services start mongodb-community@7.0# Start MongoDBbrew services list# Check if MongoDB is running```bash**macOS:**```net start MongoDB# Start service if stoppedGet-Service MongoDB# Check if MongoDB service is running```powershell**Windows:****Solution:**### Error: "Cannot connect to local MongoDB"```# Or visit: https://whatismyipaddress.com/curl https://ipinfo.io/ip# Get your public IP```bash3. Wait 2-3 minutes for changes to propagate2. Add your current IP address1. Go to MongoDB Atlas → Network Access**Solution:**### Error: "MongoServerError: IP address not authorized"```DATABASE_URL="mongodb+srv://user:p%40ssw0rd%23@cluster.mongodb.net/trustxdb?retryWrites=true&w=majority"# Example with special characters```bash   - `/` → `%2F`   - `#` → `%23`   - `@` → `%40`3. Check password doesn't contain special characters that need URL encoding:2. Ensure database user exists in MongoDB Atlas → Database Access1. Verify username and password in connection string**Solution:**### Error: "MongoServerError: Authentication failed"## Troubleshooting---```npx prisma db seednpm install -D ts-node```bashRun seed:```}  }    "seed": "ts-node --compiler-options {\"module\":\"CommonJS\"} prisma/seed.ts"  "prisma": {{```jsonAdd to `package.json`:```  });    await prisma.$disconnect();  .finally(async () => {  })    process.exit(1);    console.error(e);  .catch((e) => {main()}  console.log('✓ Seeded database with:', { admin, user });  });    },      role: 'USER',      password: userPassword,      name: 'Test User',      email: 'user@trustx.com',    create: {    update: {},    where: { email: 'user@trustx.com' },  const user = await prisma.user.upsert({    const userPassword = await bcrypt.hash('user123', 10);  // Create test user  });    },      role: 'ADMIN',      password: hashedPassword,      name: 'Admin User',      email: 'admin@trustx.com',    create: {    update: {},    where: { email: 'admin@trustx.com' },  const admin = await prisma.user.upsert({    const hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash('admin123', 10);  // Create admin userasync function main() {const prisma = new PrismaClient();import bcrypt from 'bcryptjs';import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';```typescriptCreate `prisma/seed.ts`:### Seed Database (Optional)```npx prisma studio# Open Prisma Studio (GUI for database)npx prisma generate# Regenerate Prisma client after schema changesnpx prisma db push# Push schema to MongoDB```bashWith MongoDB and Prisma, we use `db push` instead of `migrate`:## Running Migrations---```✔ Generated Prisma Client✔ Database schemas synchronized✔ MongoDB connection established✔ Prisma schema loaded from prisma/schema.prisma```**Expected Output:**```npx prisma db push# Test connectionnpx prisma generate# Generate Prisma clientnpm install# Install dependencies```bash### Verify Connection```DATABASE_URL="mongodb+srv://trustxadmin:YOUR_PASSWORD@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net/trustxdb?retryWrites=true&w=majority"# For MongoDB Atlas (replace with your connection string)DATABASE_URL="mongodb://localhost:27017/trustxdb"# For local MongoDB```bashCreate `.env.local` file in your project root:### Update `.env`## Connection Configuration---- ❌ No advanced monitoring- ❌ No dedicated clusters- ✅ Daily backups- ✅ Unlimited data transfer- ✅ 512 MB storage**Free Tier Limitations:**| **M10** | 10 GB | 2 GB | 2 vCPU | Unlimited | $57/month || **M5** | 5 GB | 2 GB | Shared | Unlimited | $25/month || **M2** | 2 GB | Shared | Shared | Unlimited | $9/month || **M0 (Free)** | 512 MB | Shared | Shared | Unlimited | **$0/month** ||------|---------|-----|------|----------|-------|| Tier | Storage | RAM | vCPU | Transfer | Price |### MongoDB Atlas Pricing```mongodb+srv://trustxadmin:YOUR_PASSWORD@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net/trustxdb?retryWrites=true&w=majority```7. Add `/trustxdb` before the `?` to specify the database name:6. Replace `<password>` with your database user password```mongodb+srv://trustxadmin:<password>@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority```5. Copy the connection string:4. Driver: **Node.js**, Version: **5.5 or later**3. Choose "Connect your application"2. Click "Connect" on your cluster1. Go to "Database" in left sidebar### Step 5: Get Connection String5. Click "Confirm"4. For production: Add your server's public IP address   - ⚠️ **Security Note**: In production, whitelist specific IPs only3. For development: Click "Allow Access from Anywhere" (0.0.0.0/0)2. Click "Add IP Address"1. Click "Network Access" in left sidebar### Step 4: Whitelist IP Address7. Click "Add User"6. Database User Privileges: "Read and write to any database"5. Password: Click "Autogenerate Secure Password" and **save it securely**4. Username: `trustxadmin` (or your preference)3. Choose "Password" authentication2. Click "Add New Database User"1. Click "Database Access" in left sidebar### Step 3: Create Database User**Wait 3-5 minutes for cluster provisioning**6. Click "Create"5. Name your cluster (e.g., "Cluster0")4. Select region closest to your users for lowest latency   - Azure: Choose if using other Azure services   - Google Cloud: Good alternative   - AWS: Recommended for global apps3. Select your preferred cloud provider and region:2. Choose **FREE** M0 cluster (512 MB storage)1. Click "Build a Database"### Step 2: Create Cluster3. Choose the free M0 tier2. Sign up with email or Google/GitHub account1. Go to [mongodb.com/cloud/atlas/register](https://www.mongodb.com/cloud/atlas/register)### Step 1: Create AccountMongoDB Atlas is a fully managed cloud database service with a **free tier** (512 MB storage) perfect for development and small production apps.## MongoDB Atlas Setup---```DATABASE_URL="mongodb://localhost:27017/trustxdb"```env### Local Connection String```exit# Exitdb.test.find()# Verifydb.test.insertOne({ message: "Hello TrustX!" })# Create a test documentuse trustxdb# Create databasemongosh# Connect to MongoDB shell```bash### Verify Local Installation```mongosh --version# Verify installationsudo systemctl enable mongodsudo systemctl start mongod# Start MongoDBsudo apt-get install -y mongodb-org# Install MongoDBsudo apt-get update# Update package database   sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mongodb-org-7.0.listecho "deb [ arch=amd64,arm64 ] https://repo.mongodb.org/apt/ubuntu jammy/mongodb-org/7.0 multiverse" | \# Add MongoDB repository   sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/mongodb-server-7.0.gpgcurl -fsSL https://www.mongodb.org/static/pgp/server-7.0.asc | \# Import MongoDB public GPG key```bash#### Linux (Ubuntu/Debian)```mongosh --version# Verify installationbrew services start mongodb-community@7.0# Start MongoDBbrew install mongodb-community@7.0brew tap mongodb/brew# Using Homebrew```bash#### macOS```Get-Service MongoDB# To check status:net start MongoDB# To manually start:# MongoDB should auto-start as a Windows service```powershell**Start MongoDB:**5. Install MongoDB Compass (GUI) when prompted4. Install MongoDB as a Service (recommended)3. Choose "Complete" installation2. Run the installer (`.msi` file)1. Download MongoDB Community Server from [mongodb.com/try/download/community](https://www.mongodb.com/try/download/community)#### Windows### Installation## Local MongoDB Setup---5. [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)4. [Running Migrations](#running-migrations)3. [Connection Configuration](#connection-configuration)2. [MongoDB Atlas Setup (Cloud - Recommended for Production)](#mongodb-atlas-setup)1. [Local MongoDB Setup](#local-mongodb-setup)## Table of ContentsThis guide will help you set up MongoDB for the TrustX application, both locally and in the cloud using MongoDB Atlas.# =====================

# Database Configuration - MongoDB
# --------------------------------
# Local MongoDB (Development)
DATABASE_URL=mongodb://localhost:27017/trustxdb

# MongoDB Atlas (Production - Free Tier Available)
# DATABASE_URL=mongodb+srv://username:password@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net/trustxdb?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# Self-Hosted MongoDB (Production)
# DATABASE_URL=mongodb://username:password@your-mongodb-host:27017/trustxdb?authSource=admin

# MongoDB Atlas Connection String Format:
# 1. Sign up at https://www.mongodb.com/cloud/atlas/register
# 2. Create a cluster (Free tier available)
# 3. Database Access → Add user with password
# 4. Network Access → Add your IP (0.0.0.0/0 for development only)
# 5. Clusters → Connect → Connect your application
# 6. Copy the connection string and replace <password> with your database user password

# JWT Configuration
# IMPORTANT: Use strong random strings in production (64+ characters recommended)
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 64
JWT_SECRET=your_jwt_secret_here
REFRESH_TOKEN_SECRET=your_refresh_token_secret_here

# Optional: Token expiry times (defaults: 15m for access, 7d for refresh)
# JWT_EXPIRY=15m
# REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRY=7d

# Redis Configuration
# For local Redis server
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# For Redis Cloud or managed Redis
# REDIS_URL=redis://username:password@host:port
# For Redis with authentication
# REDIS_URL=redis://:password@localhost:6379

# Environment
NODE_ENV=development

# =====================
# Email Service Configuration (SendGrid)
# =====================
# Get your API key from: https://app.sendgrid.com/settings/api_keys
SENDGRID_API_KEY=your-sendgrid-api-key-here
SENDGRID_SENDER=noreply@yourdomain.com

# =====================
# Object Storage Configuration
# =====================
# Choose your storage provider: 'aws' or 'azure'
STORAGE_PROVIDER=aws

# AWS S3 Configuration
# --------------------
# Get credentials from AWS Console → IAM → Users
# Or run: ./scripts/setup-aws-s3.sh
AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME=your-bucket-name
AWS_REGION=us-east-1
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your-access-key-id
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your-secret-access-key

# Azure Blob Storage Configuration
# ---------------------------------
# Get credentials from Azure Portal → Storage Accounts
# Or run: ./scripts/setup-azure-blob.sh
# AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME=your-storage-account
# AZURE_STORAGE_CONTAINER_NAME=uploads
# AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_KEY=your-account-key
# AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=your-connection-string

# ============================================================================
# Secrets Manager Configuration (PRODUCTION)
# ============================================================================
# For secure cloud-based secret management
# Run setup scripts to automatically configure:
#   - AWS: ./scripts/setup-aws-secrets.sh
#   - Azure: ./scripts/setup-azure-keyvault.sh

# AWS Secrets Manager
# -------------------
# USE_SECRETS_MANAGER=true
# SECRET_NAME=nextjs/trustx-app-secrets
# SECRET_ARN=arn:aws:secretsmanager:us-east-1:123456789:secret:nextjs/trustx-app-secrets
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Azure Key Vault
# ---------------
# USE_KEY_VAULT=true
# KEYVAULT_NAME=kv-trustx-app
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=your-tenant-id

# For Service Principal Authentication (optional):
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=your-app-id
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-secret

# For Managed Identity (recommended for Azure App Service):
# Leave AZURE_CLIENT_ID and AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET empty
# Managed Identity will be used automatically

# =====================
# Client-side variables
# =====================
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com
